     "at": [
      1110,
      170
     ],
     "style": "warn",
     "title": "The wire no longer rests low",
     "text": "A released pin has only its internal pull-down, 50 to 80 kΩ. Against 2.2 kΩ it decides nothing."
    },
    {
     "from": 31.060000000000002,
     "to": 41.290000000000006,
     "anchor": [
      "c",
      "rpu"
     ],
     "at": [
      1110,
      170
     ],
     "style": "warn",
     "title": "5 V with nothing plugged in",
     "text": "The wire follows the servo supply through the resistor. One resistor on every port that may ever read such a servo."
    },
    {
     "from": 41.89,
     "to": 48.04,
     "anchor": [
      "c",
      "pad"
     ],
     "at": [
      1110,
      170
     ],
     "title": "One pin, several jobs",
     "text": "Servo pulses and DShot rest on a low wire. The pull-up serves one protocol and changes the wire for all."
    }
   ],
   "say": [
    {
     "at": 0.8,
     "text": "The textbook answer is a pull-up resistor: 2.2 kilohms from the wire to the servo's 5 volt supply."
    },
    {
     "at": 8.96,
     "text": "Pull-up and pull-down form a divider. The released wire rises to 2.9 volts, and the answer appears."
    },
    {
     "at": 17.19,
     "text": "But a resistor is there all the time, for every job of the pin. Whenever the pin lets go, at reset or between two jobs, the wire no longer rests low. It sits at 2.9 volts."
    },
    {
     "at": 30.76,
     "text": "With nothing plugged in, it is the full 5 volts. The resistor also ties the signal wire to the servo supply, and every port needs one."
    },
    {
     "at": 41.59,
     "text": "One job needs the resistor. Every other job of the pin has to live with it."
    }
   ]
  },
